-- title:
--   Monotone power comparison $\log|t| \le (\log|t|)^9$ when $\log|t| > 1$
-- statement:
--   Let $t$ be a real number with $\log |t| > 1$. Then
--   $$\log |t| \;\le\; (\log |t|)^{9}.$$
--
--   A one-line real-analysis fact: any real number exceeding $1$ is bounded by its own ninth power. It is used in the zero-free-region estimates to compare the window width $A/(\log|t|)^9$ with $A/\log|t|$, ensuring that the narrow $\log^{-9}$-neighborhood of the $1$-line on which the inverse-zeta bound is proved sits inside the wider region where the $\zeta$ and $\zeta'$ upper bounds are available.
